# ⚠ KEEP LF IN THE WORKING TREE (2026-08-21)
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is set globally on this machine, so a checkout was rewriting every source file
# to CRLF. That is harmless to Chrome and to Node at runtime — but SEVERAL UNIT TESTS SCAN SOURCE TEXT
# and anchor on "\n" (e.g. `src.indexOf("\n}")`, and the shared comment-stripper's line split). Under
# CRLF those patterns stop matching, so the suite reports failures that have nothing to do with the
# code.
#
# It cost real confusion twice in one day: both times a `git revert` / `git checkout` restored a
# known-good tree, the suite went red, and the red looked like a regression in the thing being
# reverted. The tree was byte-identical to the good commit both times — only the line endings differed.
#
# Pinning LF here fixes it once for every test and every future checkout, with no test churn and no
# dependence on a contributor's local git config. It also silences the constant
# "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings that made real warnings easy to miss.
#
# ⚠ If a test ever needs to assert on line endings, it must read bytes deliberately rather than rely
# on whatever the checkout produced.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary-ish assets: never touched, never normalized.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
